---
// Each block names the module it comes from: nothing here is typed twice.
const blocks = [
  {
    title: "Up next",
    text: "The next sessions, from the official timetable and the team's own schedule. One tap shows the whole timetable, filtered to what concerns them.",
    from: "Timetable",
    href: "/timetable/",
  },
  {
    title: "My role",
    text: "Their role and their car for this event: Race Engineer, #27, Car 27 Crew.",
    from: "Management",
    href: "/management/",
  },
  {
    title: "My accommodation",
    text: "The hotel, who they share the room with, and a link that opens it in maps.",
    from: "Logistics",
    href: "/logistics/",
  },
  {
    title: "Departure and return",
    text: "Flight, train or car, with the tickets and travel documents you attached, ready to open at the gate.",
    from: "Logistics",
    href: "/logistics/",
  },
];
---

<section class="band">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 class="title">
        Their race weekend, on one screen.
      </h2>
      <p class="lede">
        You plan the event once, in the modules you already use. Each crew member opens the event and finds their part of it first: meals and team notes included.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="grid gap-x-10 gap-y-12 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      {blocks.map((block) => (
        <li class="ruled flex flex-col">
          <h3 class="subtitle">{block.title}</h3>
          <p class="muted mt-3 leading-relaxed">{block.text}</p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-graphite">
            From <a href={block.href} class="link">{block.from}</a>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <p class="hair mt-14 max-w-3xl pt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-graphite">
      When a flight, a room or their timetable changes, the bell in their app says so.{" "}
      They read the plan: <span class="font-semibold text-ink">it stays yours</span>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
